(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-anatomy-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 4 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two rounded projections mark the distal end of the humerus at the elbow, on either side of the joint?","choices":["The greater and lesser tubercles","The medial and lateral malleoli","The greater and lesser trochanters","The medial and lateral epicondyles"],"answer":3,"exp":"The medial and lateral epicondyles are the two projections flanking the distal humerus and serve as muscle-attachment sites at the elbow. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which cartilaginous joint unites the two pubic bones anteriorly?","choices":["The sacroiliac joint","The pubic symphysis","The acetabular joint","The obturator joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two pubic bones meet anteriorly at the pubic symphysis, a cartilaginous joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"What is supination?","choices":["Crossing the radius diagonally over the ulna during rotation","Rotating the forearm bones back into the parallel anatomical position","Producing pure extension of the hand at the wrist joint","Producing pure flexion of the forearm at the elbow joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Supination is the rotation that returns the radius and ulna to their parallel, anatomical-position alignment. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which distal fibular projection forms the prominence on the lateral side of the ankle?","choices":["The medial malleolus","The head of the fibula","The tibial plateau","The lateral malleolus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula and forms the bony prominence on the lateral side of the ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which small anterior pubic projection sits just lateral to the pubic symphysis and serves as a ligament attachment?","choices":["The ischial tuberosity","The greater trochanter","The pubic tubercle","The anterior iliac crest"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pubic tubercle is a small forward-projecting bump of the pubis, lateral to the pubic symphysis, that anchors the inguinal ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What two bones make up the pectoral girdle?","choices":["The scapula and hip bone","The humerus and scapula","The clavicle and sternum","The clavicle and scapula"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pectoral girdle consists of the clavicle and scapula. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which distal posterior notch of the femur separates the medial and lateral condyles?","choices":["The greater trochanter","The head of the femur","The lateral malleolus","The intercondylar fossa"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intercondylar fossa is the deep posterior notch of the distal femur that separates the medial and lateral condyles. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is the sternal end?","choices":["The superior end","The lateral end","The medial end","The inferior end"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sternal end of the clavicle is its medial end, articulating with the sternum. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How many bones are in each free upper limb, not counting the pectoral girdle?","choices":["27 bones","32 bones","30 bones","14 bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each free upper limb has 30 bones: 1 humerus, 1 radius, 1 ulna, 8 carpals, 5 metacarpals, and 14 phalanges. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which row of hand bones lies between the carpals and the phalanges, forming the palm?","choices":["The tarsals","The cuneiforms","The sesamoids","The metacarpals"],"answer":3,"exp":"The five metacarpals lie between the carpal bones of the wrist and the phalanges of the fingers, forming the palm. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"What type of bone is the patella, and what is its function?","choices":["An irregular bone located deep within the bony pelvis","A flat bone that helps protect the abdominal organs","A large sesamoid bone that protects the knee joint","A long bone that bears the full weight of the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"The patella is a large sesamoid bone that develops within the quadriceps tendon and protects the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"With which two carpal bones does the distal end of the radius primarily articulate at the wrist?","choices":["The trapezium and the trapezoid","The scaphoid and the lunate","The capitate and the hamate","The pisiform and the triquetrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The distal radius articulates chiefly with the scaphoid and lunate of the proximal carpal row at the radiocarpal (wrist) joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which landmark is found on the inferior surface of the clavicle near its acromial (lateral) end?","choices":["Coracoid process","Sternal facet","Costal tuberosity","Conoid tubercle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The conoid tubercle is located on the inferior surface of the clavicle near the acromial (lateral) end. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which large articular surface on the medial ilium meets the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint?","choices":["The iliac fossa","The auricular surface","The acetabular fossa","The pubic crest"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ear-shaped auricular surface on the medial ilium articulates with the matching surface of the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The trochlea of the humerus articulates with which bone?","choices":["The radius","The ulna","The scaphoid","The clavicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The trochlea is the medial, pulley-shaped articular surface of the distal humerus that articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which saddle-shaped depression of the sphenoid bone houses the pituitary gland?","choices":["The cribriform plate","The mandibular fossa","The sella turcica","The pterygoid fossa"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sella turcica is the saddle-shaped depression on the body of the sphenoid that cradles the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why is C7 called the \"vertebra prominens,\" and what ligament attaches there?","choices":["It lacks a spinous process entirely, and the anterior longitudinal ligament attaches there instead","It has the largest vertebral foramen of any cervical vertebra, and the transverse ligament attaches there","It has a long, prominent spinous process where the ligamentum nuchae begins, extending to the external occipital crest","It is the only cervical vertebra bearing true costal facets, and the supraspinous ligament attaches there"],"answer":2,"exp":"C7 is called the vertebra prominens because it has a long, prominent spinous process; the ligamentum nuchae, a large elastic ligament, begins there and extends to the external occipital crest of the skull. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which two spinal curves are the primary (fetal) curves, concave anteriorly?","choices":["The thoracic and sacral curves","The cervical and lumbar curves","The cervical and thoracic curves","The lumbar and sacral curves"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thoracic and sacral curves are the primary curves, present from fetal life and concave anteriorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What passes through the mandibular foramen and mandibular canal?","choices":["The internal carotid artery, running through the base of the skull","Structures supplying the lower teeth, exiting anteriorly at the mental foramen","The optic nerve, traveling from the eye toward the brain","The facial nerve exclusively, without any accompanying blood vessels"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mandibular foramen and canal transmit the neurovascular structures supplying the lower teeth, which exit anteriorly at the mental foramen as the mental nerve/vessels. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which midline bump on the external occipital bone is a palpable muscle-attachment landmark?","choices":["The sella turcica of the sphenoid","The external occipital protuberance","The crista galli of the ethmoid","The mental protuberance of the chin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external occipital protuberance is the midline bump on the back of the occipital bone, palpable and serving as a muscle and ligament attachment. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which three regions make up the sternum from superior to inferior?","choices":["Head, neck, and shaft","Ala, promontory, and hiatus","Ramus, angle, and condyle","Manubrium, body, and xiphoid process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sternum consists of the manubrium superiorly, the body in the middle, and the xiphoid process inferiorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is a sesamoid bone, and what is an example given in the material?","choices":["A bone that develops within certain tendons to protect them from wear; the patella is an example","A tubular bone with a shaft and two ends; the humerus is the example given","A protective flat bone forming a broad plate; the cranium is the example given","A bone found exclusively within the face; the vomer is the example given"],"answer":0,"exp":"A sesamoid bone develops within certain tendons and protects them from excessive wear and tear; the patella is the example given. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The palatine processes of the maxillae form the anterior part of which structure?","choices":["The nasal septum","The orbital floor rim","The hard palate","The zygomatic arch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The palatine processes of the maxillae join in the midline to form the anterior two-thirds of the hard palate (roof of the mouth). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The vertebral arch is formed by which two paired structures?","choices":["The pedicles and the laminae","The body and the spinous process","The transverse and spinous processes","The superior and inferior bodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vertebral arch is formed by the paired pedicles anteriorly and the paired laminae posteriorly, enclosing the vertebral foramen. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How many cervical vertebrae are in the vertebral column?","choices":["12 cervical vertebrae","5 cervical vertebrae","7 cervical vertebrae","4 cervical vertebrae"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 7 cervical vertebrae, forming the bony framework of the neck. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How many fused vertebrae typically make up the coccyx?","choices":["Three to five","Six or seven","Exactly five","Exactly two"],"answer":0,"exp":"The coccyx consists of three to five fused vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Small cavities called lacunae within bone tissue house which cells?","choices":["Osteocytes","Chondrocytes","Adipocytes","Neurons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lacunae are the small cavities in the bone matrix that house osteocytes, the mature bone cells. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which cervical vertebra lacks a bifid spinous process, unlike the others?","choices":["C3","C7","C5","C1 (atlas)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spinous processes of the cervical vertebrae are bifid except for C7, which instead has one long, prominent, non-bifid spinous process. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Through which mandibular opening do vessels and a nerve emerge onto the chin region?","choices":["The supraorbital foramen","The foramen ovale","The stylomastoid foramen","The mental foramen"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mental foramen on the anterolateral body of the mandible transmits the mental vessels and nerve to the chin and lower lip. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What large opening in the occipital bone allows passage of the spinal cord?","choices":["Condylar fossa","Hypoglossal canal","Foramen magnum","Jugular foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone through which the spinal cord passes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which deep anterior arm muscle lies beneath the biceps and is considered the strongest pure flexor of the elbow?","choices":["The supinator muscle","The brachialis","The pronator quadratus","The triceps brachii"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachialis lies deep to the biceps and is the strongest pure flexor of the elbow, acting regardless of forearm position. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which posterior leg muscles insert via the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon to plantarflex the foot?","choices":["The tibialis anterior and toe extensors","The fibularis longus and brevis","The quadriceps femoris muscles","The gastrocnemius and soleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gastrocnemius and soleus join to form the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon and plantarflex the foot at the ankle. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"The anterior compartment of the arm, containing the biceps brachii, is supplied by which nerve?","choices":["The radial nerve","The musculocutaneous nerve","The axillary nerve","The obturator nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the anterior arm flexors, including the biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Where do all three heads of the triceps brachii insert?","choices":["The coracoid process of the scapula","The radial tuberosity","The olecranon of the elbow","The medial epicondyle of the humerus"],"answer":2,"exp":"All three heads of the triceps brachii (lateral, long, and medial) insert on the olecranon of the elbow. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The muscles of the leg are divided into anterior, lateral, and which third compartment?","choices":["The medial compartment","The gluteal compartment","The posterior compartment","The pelvic compartment"],"answer":2,"exp":"The leg is divided into anterior, lateral, and posterior compartments, the posterior one containing the plantarflexors of the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Weakness of the gluteus medius and minimus, which normally abduct the thigh, produces which sign when walking?","choices":["A winged scapula on the back","A wrist-drop deformity","A Trendelenburg (hip-drop) sign","A clawed-hand deformity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Weak gluteus medius and minimus fail to stabilize the pelvis, so the opposite hip drops during walking, the Trendelenburg sign. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The gluteal muscles as a group act at which joint?","choices":["The knee joint","The ankle joint","The shoulder joint","The hip joint"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gluteal muscles act at the hip joint, producing extension, abduction, and rotation of the thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The lateral compartment of the leg, which everts the foot, is supplied by which nerve?","choices":["The superficial fibular nerve","The deep fibular nerve","The posterior tibial nerve","The femoral nerve of the thigh"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve supplies the lateral leg compartment, whose fibularis muscles evert the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which muscle produces plantar flexion and inversion of the foot, running deep in the posterior leg?","choices":["Tibialis anterior","Tibialis posterior","Extensor hallucis longus","Fibularis longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tibialis posterior produces plantar flexion and inversion of the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the \"action line\" of a muscle?","choices":["The path traced by the nerve that supplies the contracting muscle","The direction the muscle moves upon developing tension when it contracts","The line marking exactly where a muscle originates on the bone","The boundary separating the superficial from the deep muscle layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"When a muscle contracts, it develops tension, and the direction the muscle moves upon developing tension is known as the action line. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which intrinsic hand muscles are responsible for flexion of the digits and are named for their unique, worm-like shape?","choices":["Palmaris brevis","Palmar interossei","Lumbricals","Dorsal interossei"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lumbricals are responsible for flexion of the digits. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Muscles that produce the same movement at a joint are functionally described as what to each other?","choices":["Antagonists opposing the movement","Fixators of distant joints","Sphincters closing an opening","Synergists (agonists working together)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Muscles that cooperate to produce the same movement are synergists, or agonists working together at a joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What four muscles are collectively called the quadriceps femoris?","choices":["Vastus intermedius, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and rectus femoris","Rectus femoris, biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus","Vastus intermedius, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and sartorius","Vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, gracilis, and rectus femoris"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three vastus muscles (intermedius, lateralis, medialis) and the rectus femoris muscle are collectively called the quadriceps femoris. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which deep posterior leg muscle inverts the foot and supports the medial arch, inserting on several tarsal bones?","choices":["The tibialis anterior","The tibialis posterior","The fibularis brevis","The extensor hallucis longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tibialis posterior, deep in the posterior leg, inverts the foot and supports the medial longitudinal arch. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Into what four groups are the muscles that move the thigh divided?","choices":["Flexor group, extensor group, abductor group, and adductor group","Anterior group, posterior group, medial group, and lateral group","Superficial group, intermediate group, deep group, and reflected group","Gluteal group, lateral rotator group, adductor group, and iliopsoas group"],"answer":3,"exp":"Muscles that move the thigh originate on the pelvis and are divided into four groups: the gluteal group, lateral rotator group, adductor group, and iliopsoas group. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which intercostal muscles elevate the ribs to assist inspiration?","choices":["The internal intercostals","The transversus abdominis","The levator ani","The external intercostals"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external intercostal muscles elevate the ribs during inspiration, increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle moves the tongue outward and downward?","choices":["Hyoglossus","Styloglossus","Palatoglossus","Genioglossus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Genioglossus moves the tongue out and down. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What happens to thoracic cavity volume when the diaphragm contracts, and what respiratory phase does this correspond to?","choices":["The diaphragm lowers, decreasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to exhalation","The diaphragm rises, increasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to exhalation","The diaphragm lowers, increasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to inhalation","The diaphragm rises, decreasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to inhalation"],"answer":2,"exp":"When the diaphragm contracts, it lowers, increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity, which corresponds to inhalation. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The three pharyngeal constrictor muscles function mainly to do what during swallowing?","choices":["Squeeze food downward into the esophagus","Elevate the eyebrows in surprise","Rotate the eyeball outward","Compress the cheek against teeth"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior, middle, and inferior pharyngeal constrictors contract in sequence to squeeze the food bolus down toward the esophagus during swallowing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"When the diaphragm contracts, its dome does what to draw air into the lungs?","choices":["It flattens and descends","It rises and dome-shapes upward","It rotates to one side","It splits into two halves"],"answer":0,"exp":"On contraction the diaphragm's dome flattens and descends, enlarging the thoracic cavity and drawing air into the lungs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is the action of the transversus thoracis muscle?","choices":["Depresses the ribs","Compresses the abdomen","Elevates the ribs","Extends the vertebral column"],"answer":0,"exp":"Transversus thoracis depresses the ribs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which muscle encircles the mouth and purses or closes the lips, the 'kissing muscle'?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi","The orbicularis oris","The buccinator muscle","The occipitofrontalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The orbicularis oris encircles the mouth and closes and purses the lips. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The levator ani is made of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and which third part?","choices":["The masseter","The genioglossus","The soleus","The iliococcygeus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The levator ani comprises the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus, which together form most of the pelvic diaphragm. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the sarcolemma?","choices":["A bundle of muscle fibers","The connective tissue surrounding the entire muscle","The muscle cell membrane","The connective tissue surrounding individual muscle fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sarcolemma is the muscle cell membrane. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The four muscles of mastication are innervated by a branch of which cranial nerve?","choices":["The trigeminal nerve","The facial nerve","The vagus nerve","The abducens nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve five). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which is the deepest of the flat abdominal muscles, with horizontally running fibers that compress the abdomen?","choices":["The transversus abdominis","The external oblique","The rectus abdominis","The erector spinae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The transversus abdominis is the deepest flat abdominal muscle; its transverse fibers compress and support the abdominal contents. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which paired muscle of mastication, deep to the ramus, elevates the mandible along with the masseter?","choices":["The medial pterygoid","The lateral pterygoid","The occipitofrontalis","The orbicularis oculi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial pterygoid lies deep to the mandibular ramus and elevates the mandible, working with the masseter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Muscles of the axial group include those of facial expression, mastication, the neck, and which body wall region?","choices":["The forearm and hand","The trunk and abdomen","The gluteal region","The leg and foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Axial muscles include those of facial expression, mastication, the neck, and the trunk, including the abdominal and back walls. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"The suprahyoid muscles lie in which position relative to the hyoid bone?","choices":["Above the hyoid bone","Below the hyoid bone","Inside the thoracic cavity","Within the orbit of the eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suprahyoid muscles lie above the hyoid bone and elevate it or depress the mandible during swallowing and speech. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The intercostal muscles occupy the spaces between which structures?","choices":["The ribs","The vertebrae","The tarsal bones","The phalanges"],"answer":0,"exp":"The intercostal muscles fill the intercostal spaces between adjacent ribs and act on them during breathing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
